/**
 * @page py_quickstart Getting Started & Configuration
 *
 * @tableofcontents
 *
 * @section py_install Installation
 *
 * Install pre-built binary wheels from PyPI (Linux, macOS, Windows):
 *
 * @code{.sh}
 * pip install qoro-maestro
 * @endcode
 *
 * Or build from source from the repository root:
 *
 * @code{.sh}
 * pip install .
 * @endcode
 *
 * **Supported platforms (pre-built wheels):**
 *
 * | Platform | Architecture | Python |
 * |----------|-------------|--------|
 * | Linux | x86_64 | 3.10, 3.11, 3.12 |
 * | macOS | arm64 (Apple Silicon) | 3.10, 3.11, 3.12 |
 * | Windows | AMD64 | 3.10, 3.11, 3.12 |
 *
 * ---
 *
 * @section py_config SimulatorConfig — Shared Configuration
 *
 * All execution and estimation functions accept a `SimulatorConfig` object
 * that bundles every simulator knob into a single, reusable value.
 * Create one config and pass it to every call — no need to repeat
 * `simulator_type`, `simulation_type`, `max_bond_dimension`, etc.
 *
 * @code{.py}
 * import maestro
 *
 * # Create once, reuse everywhere
 * config = maestro.SimulatorConfig(
 *     simulator_type=maestro.SimulatorType.QCSim,
 *     simulation_type=maestro.SimulationType.MatrixProductState,
 *     max_bond_dimension=64,
 *     singular_value_threshold=1e-10,
 * )
 *
 * result = maestro.simple_execute(qasm, config=config, shots=1024)
 * estimate = maestro.simple_estimate(qasm, "ZZ;XX", config=config)
 * fidelity = maestro.mirror_fidelity(qc, config=config, shots=10000)
 * @endcode
 *
 * @subsection py_config_params SimulatorConfig Parameters
 *
 * | Parameter | Type | Default | Description |
 * |-----------|------|---------|-------------|
 * | `simulator_type` | `SimulatorType` | `QCSim` | Simulation backend |
 * | `simulation_type` | `SimulationType` | `Statevector` | Simulation method |
 * | `max_bond_dimension` | `int` or `None` | `None` | Bond dimension limit for MPS, MPO and GPU tensor networks. For GPU MPS/MPO, `None` uses 128 for both planning and execution. Other backends retain their own defaults. |
 * | `singular_value_threshold` | `float` or `None` | `None` | Truncation threshold for MPS, MPO and GPU tensor networks, read according to `truncation_mode` |
 * | `truncation_mode` | `str` or `None` | `None` (backend default: `"discarded_weight"`) | How `singular_value_threshold` is interpreted: `"discarded_weight"` (Qiskit Aer/ITensor convention — discard the smallest singular values until their cumulative squared weight reaches the threshold) or `"relative_max"` (discard singular values below `threshold * sigma_max`). Because the first compares squared weights, the same threshold truncates much harder under it. Only the QCSim and GPU backends support `"relative_max"`; Aer raises `ValueError` if it's requested. |
 * | `precision` | `str` or `None` | `None` | `"single"` or `"double"` for Qiskit Aer and the GPU simulators; `None` keeps each backend's default. Other backends ignore it; QCSim always uses double precision. |
 * | `disable_optimized_swapping` | `bool` | `False` | Disable swap-cost and initial qubit-map optimisation |
 * | `lookahead_depth` | `int` | `-1` | Swap optimization lookahead depth; `-1` uses the default |
 * | `mps_sampling` | `str` | `"probabilities"` | MPS shot sampling: `"probabilities"` samples without collapsing the state, `"apply_measure"` measures and restores it per shot |
 * | `seed` | `int` or `None` | `None` | Seed all backend and Maestro sampling RNGs. Parallel jobs derive deterministic, distinct child streams. |
 * | `gpu_device` | `int` or `None` | `None` | CUDA-visible device ordinal for single-GPU execution |
 * | `distributed_options` | `dict[str, str]` | `{}` | Distribution controls for `DistributedGpu` and `DistributedMpiGpu` backends (e.g. `distributed_devices`, `distributed_global_qubits`, `mpi_communicator`, `mpi_p2p_bits`). See @ref py_hpc for details. |
 * | `mps_svd_solver`, `mpo_svd_solver`, `tensor_network_svd_solver` | `str` or `None` | `None` | GPU SVD solver: `"gesvd"`, `"gesvdj"`, `"gesvdp"` or `"gesvdr"` (see @ref py_mps_gpu_svd) |
 * | `mpo_kraus_completeness_check` | `str` or `None` | `None` | MPO handling of incomplete Kraus sets: `"ignore"`, `"warn"` or `"strict"` |
 * | `mpo_restore_trace_after_truncation`, `mpo_hermitize_after_truncation` | `bool` | `False` | Renormalise the CPU MPO after each truncation |
 * | `pp_coefficient_threshold` | `float` or `None` | `None` | Pauli propagation: drop strings whose \|coefficient\| is at most this |
 * | `pp_max_pauli_weight` | `int` or `None` | `None` | Pauli propagation: drop strings acting on more qubits than this |
 * | `pp_gates_between_trims`, `pp_gates_between_deduplications` | `int` or `None` | `None` | Pauli propagation: how often (in primitive operations) to apply the two thresholds; a deduplication pass also merges repeated strings. Unset, PauliPropagator simulations deduplicate every 10 operations. |
 * | `path_integral_threshold` | `float` or `None` | `None` | PathIntegral trim threshold |
 *
 * The constructor takes every parameter by keyword only.
 *
 * @note When no `config` is passed, a default `SimulatorConfig()` is used
 * (QCSim + Statevector with all defaults). You can also modify fields
 * after construction:
 *
 * @code{.py}
 * config = maestro.SimulatorConfig()
 * config.simulation_type = maestro.SimulationType.MatrixProductState
 * config.max_bond_dimension = 128
 * @endcode
 *
 * ---
 *
 * @section py_quick Quick Start — One-Line Execution
 *
 * The fastest way to run a circuit is `maestro.simple_execute`.
 * Pass an OpenQASM 2.0 string and get measurement counts back immediately.
 *
 * @code{.py}
 * import maestro
 *
 * qasm = """
 * OPENQASM 2.0;
 * include "qelib1.inc";
 * qreg q[2];
 * creg c[2];
 * h q[0];
 * cx q[0], q[1];
 * measure q -> c;
 * """
 *
 * result = maestro.simple_execute(qasm, shots=1024)
 *
 * print(result["counts"])      # e.g. {"00": 512, "11": 512}
 * print(result["simulator"])   # e.g. "QCSim"
 * print(result["method"])      # e.g. "Statevector"
 * print(f"{result['time_taken']:.4f}s")
 * @endcode
 *
 * **Result Dictionary Schema:**
 *
 * | Key | Type | Description |
 * |-----|------|-------------|
 * | `counts` | `dict[str, int]` | Measurement outcome -> count |
 * | `simulator` | `int` | Backend enum value (see `SimulatorType`) |
 * | `method` | `int` | Simulation method enum value (see `SimulationType`) |
 * | `time_taken` | `float` | Wall-clock execution time in seconds |
 */
